mrs. Bush Jenna Bush's rejoin you from  the National Book Festival have we  become a nation of readers well I hope  we're a nation of readers and I think  today it certainly looks like it on the  National Mall the National Book Festival  it attracts over a hundred thousand  people and I hope that many will come  today and come down here and meet their  favorite authors sure there are a lot of  people that don't read obviously we know  from a report that the National  Endowment for the Arts did a study that  they did that say there's a literacy in  America not illiteracy not people who  don't know how to read but people who  know how to read but don't and one of  the things one of the reasons we have a  National Book Festival and one of the  things that both Jenna and I've worked  on our whole lives really is making sure  people do read because reading and being  able to read well and comprehend is such  a huge advantage for people people who  can do that can get a job and can live a  much more successful life than people  who can't read in general from your  perspective yes I mean I think we are  working in schools teaching language  arts in English so is a biased English  teacher I hope we are I think you know  the perspective that adults must do  whether you're parents or teachers or  librarians or just role models is find  books that are interesting to kids  don't just feed the literature that you  loved as a child down their throats but  find things that interest them and I  found especially with boys that graphic  novels which are very popular in schools  now and comics and RL Stein who is the  god of scary stories for little boys as  long as my kids are reading I think  we're a nation of readers and I think  literacy as books are becoming more and  more popular in schools what were your  reading habits when the girls were young  for you and your husband well we've  always read I mean I'm a librarian of  course I've read since I learned to read  my mother was a huge reader and read to  me before I knew how to read and then  George and I both read to Jenna and  Barbara and now we trade books Jenna and  Barbara and I do recommend our favorite  books to each other and so it really is  intergenerational it's very important  for parents to let children know that  reading is important and to spend those  few years those just brief years that  you have when you have little children  at home before they go to school spend  time with them with your arm around them  reading to them it's there's no more  important time and it's something that  children will grow up and remember and  it's a wonderful memory for parents as  well remembering what it was like to  share those funny dr. Seuss books with  their kids or Curious George or all  those others that are classics of  American literature let me ask you about  the collaborative effort that you had in  writing the book what was it like for  you and and I know you were involved in  the process it was a lot of fun it was  really interesting you know after 26  years of having a relationship as a  mother  daughter and that type of relationship  to work creatively with my mom because  it's a relationship and you don't get to  experience very often but it was we had  a great time and I think really we  bounced ideas off of each other  often and it happened really naturally  and organically and it's based on  stories that she told me because you  know what books are especially you know  fiction and nonfiction or great stories  and if they didn't read they were  telling us stories and of her time  teaching because as little girls we were  fascinated with what it would be like to  be teachers have you thought about  whether you and the president will  collaborate on a book I think we will  probably write books after we leave I  mean I'm not saying for sure but I don't  know if we'll work together on one but  that's not a bad idea what about your  experiences will you write a book about  being first lady well I don't know that  for sure but I hope so this book  festival now in its eighth year will it  continue I don't know I think that I  think that it will at such an  institution people love it so many  people come out I think authors love to  be here on the National Mall they love  to meet each other and be together here  and so I hope that it will continue and  I think it will I believe it's a become  an institution in Washington and so I I  want to encourage you ever comes after  us to pay attention to it and make sure  the National Book Festival continues  what have you learned during this  process in organizing this with the  Library of Congress well I've learned  how fabulous our Library of Congress is  I don't know people really are aware if  most Americans are aware that our  Library of Congress is the largest  library in the world and they have a  copy of everything that's published in  the United States and nearly everything  that's published worldwide they're  working right now on a digital library  to put every single thing in the Library  of Congress on the web digitally which  will be really a wonderful treasure for  generations after this to be able to  access everything that's here but I also  have learned that people love books that  they love to come down to meet their  favorite authors that they're willing to  stand in line  get a autograph from their favorite  writers that they want to hear stories  just like Jenna said I think we all know  that all of us love a good story and  that's what I think this really proves  that people want to come here great  stories from our great American writers  and you touched on this earlier but what  makes a good book what makes a good  story well anything that I think is  human in a way that there are ways we  can relate to it it could be a true  story a story of real people or a story  of real animals or a story of something  that is real in our world or it could be  totally imaginary totally fantasy and  totally fiction I think what makes a  good story is a story that that somehow  people can relate to as they read it  even if it's total fantasy or fiction  like for instance the great Harry Potter  books that are totally fantasy but  they're fascinating stories for me I  mean I think it depends on the person I  mean I think real characters characters  that we can relate with characters that  you can see yourself in or our  characters that can make you lose  yourself you know I mean it depends on  the person though last question for you  in a couple of months you will be  leaving Washington going back to Texas  what's next for you and what has the  last eight years meant to you well it's  been unbelievable privilege to be here  and to be in Washington and to represent  the people of the United States like  I've had the chance to do representing  them overseas and in a lot of countries  around the world I'll continue to work  on the same things I've always worked on  on libraries and literacy I'm still the  honorary ambassador for the UN decade of  literacy and so I'll continue to work  with the UN focusing on the 34 countries  around the world that have the highest  rates of illiteracy because I believe  that literacy worldwide and especially  for girls and women who are the ones  most often denied education that  literacy worldwide will go a long way to  solving a lot of our problems  and will you write another book I hope  so I mean right now I'm teaching but  kids say the most inspiring things so I  hope I continue to teach and write and  see Jenna Bush mrs. Bush thank you for  your state thanks Trish I did
 

